Where the copyleft code runs
----------------------------

Copyleft components run as separate processes or containers, with two
exceptions, both inside the bb84-kme service's own Python process, that a
redistributor has to know about:

  * SimQN (GPL-3.0) is imported into that process.
      - services/bb84-kme/Dockerfile runs
        `pip install -c constraints.txt -e /opt/SimQN`, so it is baked into the
        image;
      - services/bb84-kme/app/backends/simqn_backend.py imports `qns` and
        `qns.entity.QNode` and runs them in-process, not over a socket;
      - config/qkd_params.yaml sets `backend: "simqn"` as the DEFAULT, so the
        default build exercises it.
    Treat services/bb84-kme as a GPL-3.0 sub-component and be prepared to make
    the corresponding source offer if you hand that image to a third party.
    Selecting SIMULATOR_BACKEND=cvqkd or qutip stops SimQN being INVOKED; it
    does not remove it from an image already built.

  * gmpy2 (LGPL-3.0-or-later) is imported into that process by SeQUeNCe.
    Redistributing the image means carrying the LGPL texts and a source offer
    for the bundled GMP, MPFR and MPC libraries; because they are shared
    objects, the relinking condition is met.

qkdnetsim and ns-3 (both GPL-2.0-only) are compiled by
services/qkdnetsim-kme/Dockerfile into an optional image but never executed,
and the strongSwan lane runs in its own containers. That separation is what
keeps the rest of the combination distributable, so it is a constraint on how
this project may be integrated rather than a footnote: GPL-2.0-only code cannot
be combined into one work with the GPL-3.0 SimQN or with the Apache-2.0 code.
See docs/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md for per-dependency detail, including the npm
